{
  "concept": "Snell's law n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ governs how a light ray bends at an interface — toward the normal when entering a denser medium, away from it when entering a rarer one, and into total internal reflection past the critical angle when n₁ > n₂.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "horizontal black interface line separating the two media",
    "upper medium label n₁ (left of the normal, above the interface)",
    "lower medium label n₂ (left of the normal, below the interface)",
    "thin vertical surface normal line through the point of incidence, labeled 'surface normal'",
    "small right-angle square marker between the normal and the interface",
    "thick blue incident ray arriving from the upper left, labeled 'ray direction'",
    "thick blue refracted ray continuing into the lower medium, bent relative to the incident direction",
    "thick blue reflected ray (visible only during total internal reflection), symmetric to the incident ray about the normal in the upper medium",
    "angle arc θ₁ between the incident ray and the normal, with italic label θ₁",
    "angle arc θ₂ between the refracted ray and the normal, with italic label θ₂",
    "live readout of θ₁ and θ₂ in degrees near the arcs",
    "critical-angle indicator/readout shown when n₁ > n₂"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "dragIncidentRay",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "Angles are measured from the surface normal, and θ₂ is not independent — as the incident ray rotates about the interface point, the refracted ray re-aims itself continuously so that n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂ always holds"
    },
    {
      "id": "indexN1",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "n₁ (incident medium)",
      "range": [1.0, 2.0, 0.01],
      "default": 1.0,
      "teaches": "Raising n₁ relative to n₂ makes the ray bend away from the normal and is the precondition for total internal reflection"
    },
    {
      "id": "indexN2",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "n₂ (transmitting medium)",
      "range": [1.0, 2.0, 0.01],
      "default": 1.5,
      "teaches": "Raising n₂ relative to n₁ bends the refracted ray closer to the surface normal; lowering it does the opposite"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Bending toward the normal",
      "narration": "With n₁ = 1.0 (air) and n₂ = 1.5 (glass), the ray enters a denser medium and bends toward the normal — θ₂ < θ₁. Drag the incident ray and watch θ₂ track θ₁ exactly as Snell's law demands.",
      "state": { "indexN1": 1.0, "indexN2": 1.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Stronger contrast, stronger bend",
      "narration": "Increase n₂ toward 2.0: the refracted ray hugs the normal ever more closely for the same θ₁ — the amount of bending is set by the ratio of indices, not by either one alone.",
      "state": { "indexN1": 1.0, "indexN2": 2.0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Bending away from the normal",
      "narration": "Swap the contrast — n₁ = 1.8, n₂ = 1.0. Now light speeds up crossing the interface and the refracted ray bends away from the normal, so θ₂ > θ₁.",
      "state": { "indexN1": 1.8, "indexN2": 1.0 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Total internal reflection",
      "narration": "With n₁ > n₂, drag θ₁ past the critical angle θc = arcsin(n₂/n₁): no real θ₂ can satisfy Snell's law, so the refracted ray vanishes and a reflected ray appears symmetrically in the upper medium — all the light stays inside.",
      "state": { "indexN1": 1.8, "indexN2": 1.0 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "θ₂ is slaved to θ₁ by n₁ sin θ₁ = n₂ sin θ₂, so when n₁ > n₂ there is a maximum θ₁ — the critical angle — beyond which no refracted ray can exist and light reflects totally inside the denser medium.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Match the textbook layout: horizontal black interface centered vertically, thin vertical normal through the incidence point with a right-angle square in the upper-right quadrant, 'surface normal' label at the top of that line, n₁ and n₂ labels stacked on the left side, incident ray entering from the upper left with the rotated 'ray direction' label alongside it, refracted ray exiting lower right and bent toward the normal in the default state (n₁=1.0, n₂=1.5). Rays are thick blue; the normal is a thinner, lighter blue. θ₁ and θ₂ are arc markers with italic serif labels placed between ray and normal. Dragging rotates the incident ray about the fixed incidence point (clamp θ₁ to roughly 0–90°); θ₂, its arc, and the refracted ray update live from Snell's law. When n₁ > n₂, show a critical-angle readout and a faint dashed marker at θc; once θ₁ exceeds θc, fade out the refracted ray and θ₂ arc, and fade in a reflected ray mirrored across the normal, optionally labeled 'total internal reflection'. At θ₁ = 0 the ray passes straight through undeviated — a useful anchor state. Keep the two index sliders visibly paired with their n₁/n₂ labels in the scene."
}

